58:["$","p",null,{"children":["We compared both algorithms on an Apple M2 Max with 12 CPU cores and 32 GB of\nmemory, running macOS 15.6 and Bazel 9.1.1. Each of two alternating runs built\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"//:forge"}]," against a fresh local Forge filesystem cache with hash and\nsize verification enabled. Uploads were synchronous, and the Bazel output and\nForge cache directories were isolated by algorithm."]}]
59:["$","div",null,{"className":"relative overflow-auto prose-no-margin my-6","children":["$","table",null,{"children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","th",null,{"children":"Phase"}],["$","th",null,{"children":"SHA-256 runs"}],["$","th",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"SHA-256 mean"}],["$","th",null,{"children":"BLAKE3 runs"}],["$","th",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"BLAKE3 mean"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","td",null,{"children":"Compile and populate an empty cache"}],["$","td",null,{"children":"179.90 s, 180.18 s"}],["$","td",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"180.04 s"}],["$","td",null,{"children":"181.56 s, 185.57 s"}],["$","td",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"183.57 s"}]]}],["$","tr",null,{"children":[["$","td",null,{"children":"Rebuild from the populated cache"}],["$","td",null,{"children":"6.39 s, 5.93 s"}],["$","td",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"6.16 s"}],["$","td",null,{"children":"6.74 s, 6.41 s"}],["$","td",null,{"style":{"textAlign":"right"},"children":"6.58 s"}]]}]]}]]}]}]
5a:["$","p",null,{"children":"Both population builds performed 2,620 actions. Each cached rebuild reported\n1,458 remote cache hits. SHA-256 was 1.9% faster while populating the cache and\n6.3% faster on the cached rebuild in this test, so SHA-256 remains the default."}]
5b:["$","p",null,{"children":"These are whole-build measurements from one macOS machine, not a universal\nhashing benchmark; compilation, storage, scheduling, and operating-system\ncaching dominate much of the elapsed time. Measure your own representative\nworkload before changing algorithms. Keeping every participant aligned matters\nfar more than a small isolated timing difference."}]
5c:["$","$L1c",null,{"as":"h2","id":"faq","children":"FAQ"}]
5d:["$","$L6f",null,{"children":[["$","$L70",null,{"title":"What happens when a store fills up?","id":"what-happens-when-a-store-fills-up","children":["$","p",null,{"children":[["$","code",null,{"children":"eviction_policy.max_bytes"}]," caps it, and once full the least-recently-used\nentries are evicted to make room. An evicted CAS entry is just a future cache\nmiss; the client rebuilds and re-uploads. An evicted entry that a cached\naction result still points at is the problem ",["$","code",null,{"children":"completeness_checking"}]," exists to\nsolve."]}]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Do I need a separate store for the CAS and the action cache?","id":"do-i-need-a-separate-store-for-the-cas-and-the-action-cache","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":["Yes, and the config loader enforces it: if a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"cas"}]," and an ",["$","code",null,{"children":"ac"}]," entry with the\nsame ",["$","code",null,{"children":"instance_name"}]," name the same store, startup fails with ",["$","code",null,{"children":"CAS and AC use the same store '...' in the config"}],". Beyond that check, they have very\ndifferent shapes. The CAS holds many large-ish blobs and wants compression and\ndeduplication; the AC holds small metadata records, wants none of that, and\nwants ",["$","code",null,{"children":"completeness_checking"}],"."]}],["$","a",null,{"href":"https://github.com/toxicwind/forge/blob/v1.6.5/forge-config/src/cas_server.rs","target":"_blank","rel":"noreferrer","data-source-link":"forge-config/src/cas_server.rs","data-source-ref":"v1.6.5","title":"forge-config/src/cas_server.rs at v1.6.5","className":"inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md border border-border bg-surface-elevated px-1.5 py-0.5 align-middle font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground no-underline transition-colors hover:border-brand/50 hover:text-brand","children":[["$","svg",null,{"width":"12","height":"12","viewBox":"0 0 24 24","fill":"none","stroke":"currentColor","strokeWidth":"2","strokeLinecap":"round","strokeLinejoin":"round","aria-hidden":"true","children":["$","path",null,{"d":"m16 18 6-6-6-6M8 6l-6 6 6 6"}]}],"check_store_conflict"]}]]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Why is `fast_slow` required for workers?","id":"why-is-fast_slow-required-for-workers","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["A worker materialises action inputs onto disk by hard-linking out of its\nstore, which only works if the store is filesystem-backed. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"fast_slow"}]," with a\n",["$","code",null,{"children":"filesystem"}]," fast tier gives it that, and lets the slow tier be a shared\nremote CAS, or a ",["$","code",null,{"children":"noop"}],", when the fast tier ",["$","em",null,{"children":"is"}]," the storage. ",["$","code",null,{"children":"basic_cas.json5"}],"\ntakes the second option."]}]}],["$","$L70",null,{"title":"Can two Forge processes share one filesystem store?","id":"can-two-forge-processes-share-one-filesystem-store","children":["$","p",null,{"children":["No. A ",["$","code",null,{"children":"filesystem"}]," store owns its directories: it scans them at boot and\nmanages eviction against its own view of what's there. Two processes pointed\nat the same paths will evict each other's data. Share by putting a real shared\nbackend behind them (object storage, Redis) or by having one process serve the\nCAS over gRPC to the others."]}]}]]}]
